\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A triangulation here is a cellular embedding of a finite graph in a closed surface with every face bounded by a walk of length three. Its dual $G^*$ has a vertex for each face and one edge crossing each primal edge. A proper $3$-edge-colouring assigns three colours to edges, with incident edges having different colours. Face-width is the least number of intersection points with the embedded graph among noncontractible simple closed curves on the surface. A cubic graph has degree three at every vertex; in the convention of this source, a snark is a cubic graph which is not $3$-edge-colourable and is internally $4$-edge-connected (no edge cut of size at most three separates two parts each having at least two vertices).
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
The original Grünbaum assertion says that if $G$ is a simple loopless triangulation of an orientable surface, then $G^*$ has a proper $3$-edge-colouring. The source gives its snark formulation: every orientable cellular embedding of a snark has a loop or parallel edges in the dual. It also records the following weaker questions: (i) is there an absolute constant $k$ such that the dual of every loopless orientable triangulation of face-width greater than $k$ is $3$-edge-colourable; (ii) does that assertion extend to nonorientable surfaces; and (iii) for every orientable surface $\Sigma$, is there a constant $k(\Sigma)$ giving this conclusion for loopless triangulations of $\Sigma$? For comparison, the separately named orientable cycle-double-cover conjecture in the source asks whether every snark has an orientable cellular embedding with a loopless dual. \href{https://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/grunbaums_conjecture}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
The historical conjecture concerns three-edge-colourability of duals of simple orientable triangulations. The source also asks weaker versions with large face-width, either uniformly or depending on the surface, and discusses snark embeddings.

